<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	Movena links against libmpv, which most users get via Homebrew rather
	than a copy signed with our own identity. Ad-hoc code signing (see
	`signingIdentity` in tauri.conf.json) otherwise triggers macOS Library
	Validation, which refuses to dlopen any dylib whose signing Team ID
	doesn't match ours — that's what turns into a "Movena kann aufgrund
	eines Problems nicht geöffnet werden" crash on launch. This entitlement
	turns that check off for this app specifically.

	Once the app is signed with a real Apple Developer ID and libmpv is
	vendored into Contents/Frameworks instead of loaded from Homebrew's
	install path, this entitlement (and the Homebrew dependency itself)
	should be dropped.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	mpv's default on-screen-controller/console UI runs on LuaJIT, which
	Homebrew's libmpv links in and which JIT-compiles Lua bytecode into
	executable memory at runtime — only once playback actually starts,
	which is why this doesn't show up until then. That trips macOS's W^X
	enforcement (SIGKILL "Code Signature Invalid") unless the app is
	allowed to create executable memory outside the MAP_JIT convention,
	which is what LuaJIT uses on its own.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
